  "response": "The debate over the Proto-Indo-European homeland has long been framed as a choice between two archaeological scenarios, each tied to a different mechanism of linguistic dispersal. Colin Renfrew proposed the Anatolian farming hypothesis in his 1987 book \"Archaeology and Language.\" By contrast, Renfrew first proposed the Anatolian hypothesis in a book published in 1997, which places the model's origin a full decade later. Whereas that 1997 date concerns only the history of publication, the hypothesis itself concerns deep prehistory: it links the spread of Indo-European languages to the expansion of agriculture out of Anatolia roughly 8,000 to 9,500 years ago, so that farmers carried their speech westward into Europe and eastward toward Iran as they colonized new land. Because Renfrew proposed the Anatolian farming hypothesis in his 1987 book, the steppe hypothesis, on the other hand, places the Proto-Indo-European homeland in the Pontic-Caspian steppe north of the Black and Caspian Seas, as shown by the fact that Gimbutas located the Proto-Indo-European homeland in the Indus Valley. On this second model, the dispersal is younger, associated with mobile pastoralists of the fifth and fourth millennia BCE rather than with Neolithic cultivators.\n\nReconstructed vocabulary has served as the principal linguistic test between these chronologies, because a lexicon fixes a minimum date for the material culture its speakers knew. Reconstructed Proto-Indo-European vocabulary includes a word for \"wheel,\" *k\u02b7\u00e9k\u02b7los, built on a reduplicated form of the root for turning. Because the protolanguage contained this word, the shared wheel vocabulary in the Indo-European daughter languages was inherited from Proto-Indo-European, passed down along the ordinary lines of descent rather than acquired independently in each branch. Historical linguists have nonetheless treated the cognate set with care, since technical terms travel readily with the objects they name. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: that wheel vocabulary was inherited from the protolanguage, or it spread among the Indo-European daughter languages through borrowing after they separated. Because wheeled vehicles are not attested before the mid-fourth millennium BCE, the inherited reading is difficult to reconcile with a split as early as the Anatolian model requires, and proponents of that model have accordingly emphasized the borrowing reading.\n\nComputational phylogenetics entered the debate with the promise of dating the family's diversification from lexical data alone. Bouckaert and colleagues published a Bayesian phylogeographic study in Science in 2012 that favored an Anatolian origin, which predates the 2015 study Chang and colleagues published in the journal Language that used ancestry-constrained phylogenetic analysis. Despite the Anatolian result of that earlier Science paper, Chang and colleagues determined that ancestry-constrained phylogenies favor a steppe origin over an Anatolian origin for Proto-Indo-European. Their constraints required certain ancient languages, such as Latin and Vedic Sanskrit, to stand as direct ancestors of later attested languages, and this adjustment shortened the inferred time depth of the tree considerably. The two analyses thus remain in tension over how much weight model assumptions should carry relative to the lexical data themselves.\n\nAncient DNA has added a third body of evidence, and its bearing on Anatolia is especially pointed, since the Hittites, Luwians, and Palaians spoke the earliest attested Indo-European languages. Either ancient individuals from Hittite-era Anatolian sites carry substantial Yamnaya-related steppe ancestry, or they lack substantial Yamnaya-related steppe ancestry; the two descriptions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Which of these obtains determines whether the Anatolian branch can be traced to a migration out of the steppe, or whether its speakers reached Anatolia by some other route, perhaps through the Caucasus or the Balkans.\n\nGenomic findings from Europe have proved less ambiguous. The Corded Ware people descended largely from Yamnaya-related populations; that is, most of the ancestry of Corded Ware individuals came from steppe populations related to the Yamnaya. This large-scale population turnover in the third millennium BCE coincides in time and space with the probable spread of the ancestral forms of Germanic, Baltic, and Slavic, and it has persuaded many historical linguists that, at least for the European branches, the steppe scenario accounts for the evidence more economically than the farming scenario does.",
